@meta
id: 29-04
title: What a plugin manager actually does
chapter: 29
type: lesson
skills: plugin-manager, packadd
par: 92
minutes: 8
vscode: there is no equivalent; the marketplace is the manager
open: vimrc install.sh
vimrc: set hidden

@brief
Every vim tutorial on the internet opens with "first, install a plugin
manager". It is worth knowing what one is before you agree to it.

`vimrc` here is somebody's plugin block, written for vim-plug. Four
plugins. What vim-plug does with that block, on `:PlugInstall`, is:

  - `git clone` each repository into a directory
  - put that directory on `runtimepath`
  - run `:helptags` on its `doc/`
  - on `:PlugUpdate`, `git pull` in each one

That is the entire product. Vim 8's package system does the second step
for free, and you met the third one last lesson, which leaves `git clone`.

Write the shell script that does it. `install.sh` already has the two
lines that make the target directory and step into it. Add one
`git clone` line per plugin, in the order the vimrc lists them, of the
form:

    git clone https://github.com/tpope/vim-surround.git

The `vimrc` file is here to read. Do not change it.

@keys
| `yy` `p` | you already own this; the transform is a substitution |
| `:%s/Plug '\(.*\)'/…/` | capture the repo name out of each line |
| `:bn` `:bp` | move between the two buffers |
| `:wqa` | write and quit both |

@hints
Do not type four URLs. The four repository names are already in the file
next door, one per line, in the right order.
--
Yank the four `Plug` lines, put them at the bottom of `install.sh`, and
then turn them into clone commands with one `:%s`.
--
`/^Plug` `Enter`, `4yy`, `:bn` `Enter`, `G`, `p`, then
`:%s/Plug '\(.*\)'/git clone https:\/\/github.com\/\1.git/` `Enter`.
Forward slashes inside an `s///` have to be escaped, which is the one
ugly part.

@solution
    /^Plug<Enter>  4yy  :bn<Enter>  G  p
    :%s/Plug '\(.*\)'/git clone https:\/\/github.com\/\1.git/<Enter>
    :wqa<Enter>

Four lines of data, moved once and transformed once. Typing the four URLs
by hand is about 180 keystrokes and two typos.

Now the argument the lesson is really making. A manager buys you `git
clone`, `git pull` and `helptags`, and charges you: a bootstrapping step
in your vimrc, a dependency that can break your editor on a machine where
it did not install cleanly, a lock file, and a layer between you and
`runtimepath` when something goes wrong. For four plugins that is a bad
trade. `git clone` into `~/.vim/pack/plugins/start/` and you are done, and
your config works on a fresh machine as soon as the files are there.

Where a manager starts to earn its place: twenty or thirty plugins,
plugins with dependencies on each other, or a config you want to pin to
exact commits and reproduce on a team's machines. Then lock files and
update commands are doing real work. vim-plug is the small one, lazy.nvim
the big one on the neovim side — chapter 31.

Either way you now know what is underneath, which means when the manager
breaks you can go and look at the directory it was managing.

@start vimrc
" --- Plugins --------------------------------------------------------
call plug#begin('~/.vim/plugged')
Plug 'tpope/vim-surround'
Plug 'tpope/vim-commentary'
Plug 'tpope/vim-fugitive'
Plug 'junegunn/fzf.vim'
call plug#end()

" --- Everything else ------------------------------------------------
set number
set ignorecase
set smartcase

@start install.sh
#!/bin/sh
set -e

mkdir -p ~/.vim/pack/plugins/start
cd ~/.vim/pack/plugins/start

@expected install.sh
#!/bin/sh
set -e

mkdir -p ~/.vim/pack/plugins/start
cd ~/.vim/pack/plugins/start
git clone https://github.com/tpope/vim-surround.git
git clone https://github.com/tpope/vim-commentary.git
git clone https://github.com/tpope/vim-fugitive.git
git clone https://github.com/junegunn/fzf.vim.git
